Extract every field your tool's input_schema requires. Be specific where the image supports it; use null only where no observation is possible.

Important guidance:
- `time_of_day`: infer from sun position, sky color, and lighting (morning, midday, golden_hour, dusk, night, indoor, ambiguous).
- `mood`: a short adjective phrase capturing the dominant feeling (e.g. "calm and contemplative", "energetic crowd", "intimate quiet").
- `lighting`: describe direction + quality (e.g. "soft backlit", "harsh overhead", "warm tungsten").
- `quality`: an objective 0.0–1.0 estimate based on focus, exposure, framing, and motion blur. Reserve >0.85 for genuinely outstanding frames.
- `people.count`: number of distinct human subjects clearly visible.
- `people.in_focus`: list of short descriptors when faces are identifiable (e.g. ["adult woman center", "child left foreground"]). Empty list when no people.
- `location.description`: a one-line guess at setting type (e.g. "alpine meadow with snow-capped peaks", "coastal cafe terrace"); leave null if ambiguous.
- `foreground_activity` / `background_activity`: short verb phrases.
- `objects`: list of notable visible objects, prefixed with size class — "S:phone", "M:bicycle", "L:car".
- `clothing`: short list of distinctive clothing items if any people are present.
- `tags`: 3–8 short keyword tags useful for cross-referencing this photo.
- `shot_type`: the cinematographic shot scale — one of extreme_wide, wide, establishing, medium, close_up, extreme_close_up, aerial, macro, ambiguous. Judge by how much of the subject fills the frame.
- `main_subjects`: the people the shot is ABOUT (not incidental passers-by). For each, give `descriptor` (e.g. "adult woman, white cap, center"), `expression` (their facial expression: "broad smile", "laughing", "contemplative", "neutral", "surprised"), and `prominence` (0.0–1.0, how central they are). Empty list for pure scenery.
- `other_people`: one aggregate line describing incidental / background people, if any ("a few hikers passing behind", "dense tourist crowd"). Empty string if none.
- `scenery_description`: the natural/built environment in concrete visual terms — terrain, vegetation, water, sky, weather, structures (e.g. "red sandstone slickrock, deep canyon, clear blue sky").
- `background_description`: what sits behind the subjects (depth, landmarks, clutter).
- `camera_quality_notes`: a short textual rationale for your `quality` number — say WHY ("tack sharp, golden backlight" or "motion blur on subject, blown-out sky, tilted horizon").
- `specialness_score`: 0.0–1.0 — how intrinsically memorable / visually striking this frame is, INDEPENDENT of any brief. Reserve >0.85 for genuinely wow moments.
- `safety_level`: "safe" (shareable), "mild" (mild vulgarity/suggestive), or "explicit" (nudity/graphic). Default "safe".
- `obstruction_level` + `obstruction_notes`: 0.0–1.0 for how much an obstruction or non-value crowd blocks the intended subject, plus a short note ("foreground stranger crosses subject", "finger over lens", "harsh lens flare"). 0.0 + "" when the shot is clean.

Submit your answer by calling the `submit_metadata` tool exactly once. Do not include any free-text reply.
